# {{ distribution }} is ready

git-tpl renders files and executes nothing (ADR-019), so three things are left
to you.

## 1. Install

```bash
cd {{ distribution }}
mise install
{% if is_pdm %}pdm install -G:all{% else %}mise run install{% endif %}
```

`mise install` installs the toolchain and wires the prek hooks automatically.
Its postinstall hook also installs dependencies when `{% if is_pdm %}pdm.lock{% else %}uv.lock{% endif %}` exists;
on this fresh render, the second command creates that lockfile instead.
{% if is_pdm %}
PDM is this project's task host — `pdm run --list` shows the tasks. `mise.toml`
carries the toolchain and automatic setup hooks.
{% else %}
mise is this project's task host — `mise tasks` shows them.
{% endif %}

Then commit the lockfile that step produced — `{% if is_pdm %}pdm.lock{% else %}uv.lock{% endif %}` and `mise.lock`
are both part of the repository.

## 2. Push

The `origin` remote is already set to
`git@github.com:{{ owner }}/{{ distribution }}.git`. Create the repository, then:

```bash
git push -u origin main
```
{% if gh_ship %}
## 3. Configure the release

Configure the gh-ship flow on GitHub:

{% if gh_ship_token_mode is defined and gh_ship_token_mode == 'app' %}
- **A GitHub App** with `contents: write`, `pull-requests: write` and
  `actions: write`, installed on this repository. Put its client id in the
  `release` environment as the variable `APP_CLIENT_ID` and its private key as
  the secret `APP_PRIVATE_KEY`.
  gh-ship mints and refreshes tokens itself in the single `ship` job; the
  dispatched workflows mint their own App tokens for pushes and asset uploads.
{% else %}
- **Token authentication**, without a GitHub App. Workflows use a `SHIP_TOKEN`
  secret from the `release` environment when present, otherwise their default
  GitHub token. To use a fine-grained PAT, grant repository `contents`,
  `actions`, `pull-requests` and `issues` read/write permissions. A classic PAT
  needs `repo` and `workflow` scopes.
  Use a PAT when Release PRs and release-branch pushes must trigger CI:
  GitHub's default token does not trigger those runs. When using the default
  token, enable **Allow GitHub Actions to create and approve pull requests**
  under Settings → Actions → General. Organization policy must allow it too.
{% endif %}
- **Squash-merge settings**: `Default commit message` → `Pull request title`,
  with a blank body. The Release PR's title is a Conventional Commit and the
  `commit-msg` hook checks it; a default GitHub squash message is not one.
- **A `release` environment**, which holds the release credentials.
{% if pypi %}
## 4. Configure PyPI

Trusted Publishing, so no token is ever stored. On PyPI, add a publisher for:

| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Repository | `{{ owner }}/{{ distribution }}` |
| Workflow | `publish-release.yaml` |
| Environment | `release` |

The workflow filename is part of the trust policy — renaming the file breaks
publishing until PyPI is updated.
{% endif %}
{%- else %}
## 3. Configure the release

The 🚀 Release workflow needs:

- **A `release` environment** holding a `PAT` secret: a personal access token
  with `contents: write`. The default `GITHUB_TOKEN` cannot be used, because a
  push authenticated with it triggers no workflows — the tag would never build
  anything.
{% if pypi %}
## 4. Configure PyPI

Trusted Publishing, so no token is ever stored. On PyPI, add a publisher for:

| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Repository | `{{ owner }}/{{ distribution }}` |
| Workflow | `release.yaml` |
| Environment | `release` |

The workflow filename is part of the trust policy — renaming the file breaks
publishing until PyPI is updated.
{% endif %}
{%- endif %}
